From: kernel-fedora <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/gud: avoid fortified memchr() in TV mode validation

Upstream commit eab46d962980 ("drm/gud: validate TV mode names before creating
enum property") added a fortified memchr() call on a kmalloc()'d buffer:

        if (!memchr(mode, '\0', GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_NAME_LEN)) {

The fortified memchr() passes its first parameter with
__pass_dynamic_object_size(0).  In a clang ThinLTO build of the gud module the
LTO backend resolves that object size into a compile-time constant, and the
FORTIFY_SOURCE compile-time check then fires even though the buffer is large
enough, emitting a call to __read_overflow(), which carries
__compiletime_error() and is reachable through the "dontcall-error" attribute:

  ld.lld: error: call to __read_overflow marked "dontcall-error":
  detected read beyond size of object (1st parameter)
  make[6]: *** [scripts/Makefile.build:509: drivers/gpu/drm/gud/gud.o] Error 1

The same code builds fine with gcc and without LTO, so this is a FORTIFY_SOURCE
false positive exposed by ThinLTO, not a real out-of-bounds access: buf is
kmalloc(GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_MAX_NUM * GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_NAME_LEN) and each
mode slot is GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_NAME_LEN bytes.

Replace the fortified memchr() with the approach used by CachyOS's gud tree:
write the slot terminator unconditionally, which needs no object-size analysis
at all and is therefore immune to the LTO false positive.  Keep the upstream
validation semantics by rejecting a name that turns out to be oversized (no
terminator inside its slot) with -EIO, so malformed device data is never passed
on silently truncated.

Signed-off-by: kernel-fedora <noreply@localhost>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/gud/gud_connector.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/gud/gud_connector.c
@@ -399,7 +399,14 @@
 	for (i = 0; i < num_modes; i++) {
 		char *mode = &buf[i * GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_NAME_LEN];
 
-		if (!memchr(mode, '\0', GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_NAME_LEN)) {
+		/*
+		 * Writing the terminator unconditionally avoids the fortified
+		 * memchr(), whose __pass_dynamic_object_size(0) argument makes
+		 * FORTIFY_SOURCE report a false read overflow under clang ThinLTO.
+		 */
+		mode[GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_NAME_LEN - 1] = '\0';
+		if (strnlen(mode, GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_NAME_LEN) == GUD_CONNECTOR_TV_MODE_NAME_LEN) {
+			/* Oversized name: the device sent malformed data. */
 			ret = -EIO;
 			goto free;
 		}
